s are feasible with current tools. The graph has no complete artifact inventory or selected versions, role interfaces, licenses, and argument contracts for the requested sources. This change imports no facts or artifacts on their behalf. |
| #9: honesty ledger | Implemented by this change with source-owned classification, generated summaries, closed kernel cases, and executed promotion negatives. No new SDK primitive is required. |
| #10: ULEB128 | Implemented in the new LexLean varint graph: explicit U64 domain, shortest base-128 encoding, bounded prefix/exact decoding, typed errors/offsets, general round trips/size/minimality/prefix proofs, and 236 exact vectors. [Generated contract](../../UORC-VARINT.md). |
| #11: archive frame | Exact field encoding/order, integrity rules, and C.1–C.6 vectors are absent from the graph. Generated binary transport also remains a separate SDK acceptance concern. |
| #12: graph encoding/static validation | The issue names value types and an opcode range, but the source lacks the complete opcode operand/signature table and wire layout needed for exact validation. |
| #13: reference semantics | Pure mathematical authoring is supported. The complete source definitions and theorem inventory needed to establish the requested semantic/resource model are absent. |
| #14: StepMachine | Depends on complete reference semantics, resource events, release schedule, and indexed/checked generated runtime operations. The locked SDK's capability PR does not yet pass those runtime obligations. |
| #15: store and Scan | Needs exact source-owned store/Scan accounting and refinement statements, plus passing generated-runtime store tests at all requested scales. PR #72's required store probe remains unsuccessful. |
| #16: last-use/heap | Needs the complete operand/capture inventory and allocation/root event model to establish exact release safety and peak accounting. These are not defined in the current graph. |
| #17: resource ledger | The closed event vocabulary, recipes, costs, and scale-point exte(['naturalConstant','byteConstant','predicateConstant','sequenceConstant','naturalAdd','naturalSubtract','naturalMultiply','naturalDivide','naturalRemainder','naturalAnd','naturalOr','naturalXor','naturalNot','naturalShiftLeft','naturalShiftRight','naturalEqual','naturalLess','byteEqual','byteLess','naturalToByte','byteToNatural','predicateNot','predicateAnd','predicateOr','sequenceLength','sequenceIndex','sequenceSlice','sequenceConcat','sequenceEqual','singletonSequence','basisGet','storeNew','storeRead','storeWrite','choose','apply','tabulate','fold','scan'])}
TC = {'natural': 0, 'byte': 1, 'predicate': 2, 'sequence': 3, 'store': 4}
def uleb(n):
    out = bytearray()
    while True:
        if n < 128: out.append(n); return bytes(out)
        out.append(n % 128 + 128); n //= 128
def instr(t):
    op = t[0]; o = bytearray([OPC[op]])
    if op == 'naturalConstant': o += uleb(t[1])
    elif op == 'byteConstant': o.append(t[1])
    elif op == 'predicateConstant': o.append(1 if t[1] else 0)
    elif op == 'sequenceConstant': o += uleb(len(t[1])) + bytes(t[1])
    elif op == 'apply': o += uleb(t[1]) + uleb(len(t[2])) + b''.join(uleb(x) for x in t[2])
    elif op == 'tabulate': o += uleb(t[1]) + uleb(t[2]) + uleb(len(t[3])) + b''.join(uleb(x) for x in t[3])
    elif op == 'fold': o += uleb(t[1]) + uleb(t[2]) + uleb(t[3]) + uleb(len(t[4])) + b''.join(uleb(x) for x in t[4])
    else: o += b''.join(uleb(x) for x in t[1:])
    return bytes(o)
def graph_bytes(g, entry):
    o = bytearray(uleb(len(g.blocks)))
    for b in g.blocks:
        ins, ret = b.lowered()
        o += uleb(len(b.params)) + bytes(TC[t] for t in b.params) + bytes([TC[b.result]]) + uleb(len(ins)) + b''.join(instr(t) for t in ins) + uleb(ret)
    return bytes(o + uleb(entry.index))
def archive(g, entry, lengths):
    body = graph_bytes(g, entry)
    return b'UORC' + bytes([2, 1, 0, 1]) + uleb(len(lengths)) + b''.join(uleb(n) for n in lengths) + uleb(len(body)) + body
import hashlib
DOM = bytes.fromhex('554f52432d5348413235362d434841494e2d763100')
def chain(data):
    c = hashlib.sha256(DOM + b'\x00').digest()
    full = (len(data) // 64) * 64
    if len(data) % 64 == 0 and len(data) > 0:
        pass
    i = 0
    # absorbByte: a full frame is hashed when the 64th byte arrives
    while len(data) - i >= 64:
        c = hashlib.sha256(DOM + b'\x01' + c + data[i:i + 64]).digest(); i += 64
    rest = data[i:]
    return hashlib.sha256(DOM + b'\x02' + c + bytes([len(rest)]) + rest).digest()
def sealed(pre):
    return pre + chain(pre)
# The x86 branch filter (uorc's code filter) as an ir.Prog: one description that irlex compiles to
# LexLean (Uorc.Codec.X86) and irgraph to the kernel graph block that undoes it.
#
# x86 code is full of near calls and jumps (E8/E9 rel32) whose targets repeat at different offsets;
# rewriting each displacement as an absolute target makes the repeats literal. At every opcode E8 or
# E9 with four bytes after it, a displacement v whose top byte is 00 or FF (|v| < 2^24) becomes
# sext25(v + (base + i + 5)), and decoding subtracts instead; the four bytes are then skipped either
# way. The opcode bytes never change and a rewritten displacement keeps a top byte of 00 or FF, so
# both directions scan the same positions and decode(encode(x)) = encode(decode(x)) = x for every x.
# `base` places the bytes in a longer stream: a window frame is filtered at the offset that follows
# its (filtered) history, so a target reads the same in both. `hits` counts the rewritten displacements.
